# §3.3: a public agent artifact fetched by a browser from another origin MUST be
# permitted through standard cross-origin controls. The deploy target has to
# honour this file (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages) or the equivalent vercel.json
# beside it — a host reads one and ignores the other, so a rule written in only
# one of them is present or absent depending on where we deploy, silently.
# GitHub Pages cannot set a response header at all and is disqualified for that
# reason. `tests/artifacts.test.ts` holds this file and vercel.json to each other,
# path by path, because that is how the two came apart: on the deployed site the
# three artifacts below carried CORS and /broken/ did not, and nothing in the
# repository noticed.
#
# All THREE of §3.3's minimum public files, including the link map. A link map a
# caller cannot fetch cross-origin states nothing to anyone.
/agent-manifest.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60
/vouch.network.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60
/links.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60
# The three deliberately invalid networks are published SO THAT THE REFUSALS CAN
# BE WATCHED RATHER THAN TAKEN ON TRUST, and that is a claim made to a reader who
# is not on this origin. No cache header: a fixture is fetched to be refused, and
# refusing it twice should not be served from a cache either time.
/broken/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
